{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>In the evening you shall know that YHWH brought you out, etc.</b> We have already said that the manna was spiritual food and was related to the intellectual light — 'and as the light of morning, when the sun shines forth' (II Samuel 23:4) — therefore the manna was given in the morning, morning by morning, when they asked fittingly. But the quail was coarse and material food; therefore it was given in the evening, for the intellect is related to the morning and matter to the evening. And the quail was not given to them except for the moment, for they asked for it again at the graves of craving. And this parallels what we wrote above, in the portion of וַיְחִי, on the verse 'in the morning he shall eat עַד, and in the evening he shall divide spoil' (Genesis 49:27) — meaning: the necessary thing that a person asks for fittingly is given to him in the morning, with radiant countenance; and that 'he shall eat' — that is, according to his eating. And the expression עַד means that he is never lacking anything for himself, but it is given him forever and for eternity. However, 'in the evening' — that is, the superfluities that a person asks for unbecomingly — is given to him in the evening, with darkened countenance; and that is the thing which 'he shall divide' to another — 'and leave his wealth to others' (Psalms 49:11) — and it will be torn away and distanced from his acquisitions, as the connotation of 'spoil' (שָׁלָל) indicates. And furthermore, the thing was not given to him except for the moment, like the quail, which was not given except for the moment — for 'the desire of the wicked He will not give forever' (Proverbs 10:24), but today He gives them, to remove their complaint from upon Him; tomorrow 'he craves and his soul lacks' (Proverbs 13:4). And for this reason the manna descended from heaven, the place of light, and the quail rose from the earth, the place of darkness — for each one came from its source according to what it is. Therefore it is said 'and in the morning you shall see the glory of YHWH' (Exodus 16:7) — that is, radiant countenance, for YHWH appeared to them eye to eye. But in the evening He said 'and you shall know the glory of YHWH' (Exodus 16:6) — knowledge is less than seeing, like a king who gives a certain man his requests but whose face the man has not seen. Even though he knows that this gift is the king's bestowal, in any case this — 'a king in his beauty, your eyes shall behold' (Isaiah 33:17) — is certainly better than it." "And Scripture has still not explained by what means they would know in the evening that YHWH brought them out, etc., until it explained and said 'when YHWH gives you meat in the evening to eat' (Exodus 16:8) — with darkened countenance: by this you shall know that YHWH brought you out of Egypt, from the place where you sat there by the pot of meat, in order that you come to loathe the eating of superfluities to which the bestial force lusts, and that you be occupied with the perfection of the soul — and not as you said, 'Would that we had died by the hand of YHWH in the land of Egypt, when we sat by the pot of meat, when we ate bread to satiety' (Exodus 16:3), for you took us out into the wilderness. For the intent of this complaint was that in sitting by the pot of meat there is an aspect of perfection, and we — we took you out from a place where you had this perfection to a place of lack. Behold, when YHWH gives you meat in the evening — by this you shall know that it is not we who took you out; and in that He gives you in the morning bread to satiety — by this you shall see the glory of YHWH eye to eye, for He gives with radiant countenance to all who ask fittingly."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:55.856445158Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}